# EXAMPLE ONLY -- this file is NOT shipped in the image.
#
# Every address below belongs to ONE switch. Put your own in
#
#     /mnt/data/config/network.conf
#
# which is on the data partition, so it survives an upgrade, a rollback and a
# reinstall -- and is never overwritten by an image built for this model.
#
# ⚠ THIS USED TO SHIP. An image built from this directory carried one lab
# switch's management address, its management MAC and its OSPF router-id, so a
# second switch built from the same source came up as a duplicate of the first.
# That is why the real values live off the image and only a shape lives here.
#
# Network configuration for the Arista DCS-7050TX-64.
#
# Addresses for one physical switch in one lab. Shipped rather than generated
# because none of it is the vendor's: it is how this unit is cabled and
# addressed, and somebody installing NOSaic on their own 7050TX-64 will replace
# every line.
#
# ⚠ THE MANAGEMENT MAC IS HERE BECAUSE THE BOARD WILL NOT TELL US.
#
# The address lives in prefdl on an i2c SEEPROM, not in the NIC, so tg3 comes up
# with the unprogrammed Broadcom default 00:10:18:00:00:00. The predecessor
# project works around it in the bootloader -- Aboot can read prefdl, so its
# boot0 passes the real address on the kernel command line -- and NOSaic's boot0
# does not. Until prefdl is read properly this file is the only thing that knows
# it, and it is correct for exactly one switch.
#
# The sibling board has the same problem and the same workaround.

# Management. Its own /24 off the port it is cabled to on the lab's OOB switch.
#
# .2 is what EOS uses on this box and .3 is ours, deliberately kept apart: the
# two present different MACs, and a shared address leaves a stale ARP entry
# upstream every time the box changes OS -- inbound blackholes while outbound
# works, which reads exactly like a dead switch.
# The management VRF. eth0 and its routes live in table 1001, not the main
# table the datapath mirrors into the chip and routes transit traffic through.
# ssh answers on eth0 anyway: apply-network.sh turns tcp_l3mdev_accept on, so
# services listening in the default VRF accept connections from it. Needs a
# kernel with CONFIG_NET_VRF, which is outside the A/B slot -- see
# recipes/linux/config/common.fragment.
vrf mgmt table 1001
iface eth0 <address>/<prefix> mac <this switch's management MAC> vrf mgmt
route default via <gateway> vrf mgmt

# No "pin" for the build network any more. It was a static route out of
# eth0, there because OSPF learned the management subnet over the front panel
# and the box's own replies followed it through the CPU punt path. With eth0 in
# the management VRF those replies are routed in table 1001, where OSPF's
# routes never go. Traffic the switch ORIGINATES in the default VRF (an scp
# from the switch, say) still follows the main table.

# The router id, and the one address that does not depend on a link staying up.
iface lo <router-id>/32

# Transit. MTU 1600 on all three: OSPF carries the interface MTU in its Database
# Description packets and sits in ExStart for ever on a mismatch.
#
# et49 and et50 are the two 40G links to the 7050SX2, deliberately equal cost so
# they are an ECMP pair rather than primary and backup. The far ends are that
# board's et52 (.81) and et53 (.73) -- see its config/network.conf.
iface et49 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
iface et50 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600

# et52 is the 40G link to the Edgecore AS5610. Higher OSPF cost than the SX2
# pair, so it is a backup path that stays available rather than one that carries
# transit.
iface et52 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600

# ── VLANs and routed VLAN interfaces ─────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. A port is routed until a switchport line puts it in a VLAN, and
# routed again when it leaves the last one. The routed VLAN interface is
# vlan<VID>, and an iface line on it creates it. docs/vlan.md has the model.
#
#   vlan <vid>
#   switchport <port> access <vid>
#   switchport <port> trunk <vid>[,<vid>...] [native <vid>]
#   iface vlan<vid> <address>/<prefix> [mtu <n>]
#
# Reserved on this board -- the datapath's per-port VLANs: 1001-1052.
#
# vlan 10
# vlan 20
# switchport et1 access 10
# switchport et49 trunk 10,20 native 1
# iface vlan10 <address>/<prefix> mtu 1600
